# SRG-APP-000920-WSR-000320  -  CAT II
# The web server must synchronize system clocks within and between systems
# or system components.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000920-WSR-000320
group:           V-264358
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-004922
control_rev5:    SC-45

contribution:    Splunk Web has no NTP client and takes its clock from the host, so the
                 application's share is the skew bound it enforces between the search head
                 and its peers and the unambiguous time zone it applies to events and to
                 the user interface.

governs:         limits.conf - Limits on how much work searches and data processing may consume.

check:           limits.conf [search] addpeer_skew_limit present
check:           limits.conf [search] max_tolerable_skew present

external:        The host operating system's time service (NTP or chrony), which
                 synchronizes the clock Splunk Web reads
external:        The authoritative time source shared with the host and with the devices in
                 scope of coverage
external:        props.conf TZ, which is set per source type in a [<spec>] stanza and is
                 correctly left unset where the host or forwarder time zone is intended
                 (props.conf.spec:580-590), and user-prefs.conf [general] tz, which is a
                 per-user display preference that falls back to the search head time zone
                 when unset (user-prefs.conf.spec:53-58)
external:        Operating system STIG settings for the time service

citation:        SRG-APP-000920-WSR-000320 check text; SRG-APP-000086-AU-000030 check text;
                 limits.conf.spec:493-502, 529-536; props.conf.spec:580-590;
                 user-prefs.conf.spec:53-58
finding:         Splunk expresses no clock skew bound between its own components
                 (limits.conf [search] addpeer_skew_limit or max_tolerable_skew is not
                 available in the assessed configuration), so divergence between the search
                 head serving the web interface and its peers is neither refused nor
                 reported.
